# SoDAR — Soft-labeled Data Augmentation with Adaptive Reliability

Research prototype for ovarian ultrasound segmentation on **OTU_2D** with a
patient-disjoint **850 train / 150 validation / 469 test** manifest.

**Research status:** experimental, not a reproduction of a published Dice score.
The supplied notebooks have no execution logs or generated samples. This repo
reimplements the downstream study with explicit, audited inputs; it does **not**
claim to reconstruct the original DDPM checkpoint or training results.

## Research question

Can *learned weighting of diffusion-generated soft-label supervision* prevent
negative transfer and outperform a strong real-only baseline?

The original SoDA experiments were reported by the researcher to perform worse
than no augmentation and no-augmentation + horizontal flip after the patient
overlap was fixed. **SoDAR must beat these baselines; zeroing the synthetic
weight and merely matching real-only performance does not count as success.**

## Main protocol

| Group | Real image augmentation | Synthetic data | Supervision / weight |
|---|---|---|---|
| B0 | None | No | Hard BCE + Dice |
| B1 | Horizontal flip | No | Hard BCE + Dice |
| B2 | Flip + mild rotation + photometric | No | Hard BCE + Dice |
| S1 | None (B0 setting) | Same DDPM images | Hard masks / fixed lambda |
| S2 | None (B0 setting) | Same DDPM images | Soft KL / fixed lambda |
| S3 | None (B0 setting) | Same DDPM images | Soft KL / naive trainable lambda (negative control) |
| S4 | None (B0 setting) | Same DDPM images | Soft KL / meta-learned lambda |
| S5 | None (B0 setting) | Same DDPM images | Soft KL / meta lambda + entropy-based sample weights |

**Important:** S1–S5 are **not combined with Flip**. B2 is an independent
strong conventional augmentation comparison. If desired, conventional +
synthetic synergy can be a *separate*, explicitly named experiment, not an
implicit part of S1–S5.

Compare S1 and S2 on *exactly the same generated image/logit pairs* and with
the same student epochs, minibatch sizes, optimizer, and number of updates.

## Input contract

The manifest included in `manifests/` is copied unchanged from the provided
`OTU_2D_850-150-469.json`. Both layouts are supported:

**Original manifest layout** (`data.layout: manifest`):

```text
/path/to/OTU_2D/
  images/393.JPG
  annotations/393.PNG
  ...
```

**Materialized split layout** (`data.layout: split`):

```text
/path/to/dataset_split/
  train/images/393.JPG
  train/masks/393.PNG
  validation/images/...
  validation/masks/...
  test/images/...
  test/masks/...
```

Even in split mode, the JSON manifest **determines membership**; `glob()` does
not silently redefine splits. The path resolver looks up images by manifest ID.

**DDPM output** (the directory produced by `us-dakter (1).ipynb`):

```text
/path/to/generated_dataset_full/
  images/000000.jpg
  images/000001.jpg
  logits_npy/000000.npy    # float, shape (2,H,W) or (H,W,2)
  logits_npy/000001.npy
  hard_masks/000000.jpg   # optional; NOT needed for S1–S5
```

The loader can also read `soft_labels_npy/` and names like
`soft_label_000000.npy`. The code **never** silently treats logits as
probabilities: `data.synthetic_format: logits|probabilities` is explicit.
Every `.npy` must contain exactly two background/foreground channels.

The generator must have been trained **only on the 850 training cases**. A
patient-disjoint JSON does not itself prove that an older DDPM checkpoint was
free from validation/test leakage. Record the generator checkpoint identifier
and its source train-ID manifest; retrain if it overlapped held-out patients.

## Installation

Python 3.10+ recommended. PyTorch is intentionally a user-side dependency
(GPU/CUDA wheels vary by workstation/Kaggle image).

```bash
python -m pip install -e .
python -m scripts.check_env                  # inspect existing PyTorch/CUDA
python -m pip install -e '.[segformer]'       # only for SegFormer-B2
```

The base install intentionally does not auto-install or replace PyTorch GPU
wheels. Install a matching `torch` / `torchvision` pair only if absent, using
the [official PyTorch installation selector](https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/).
`torchvision` is required for the VGG16-based SovaSeg-Net; pretrained weights
may need Internet access or a populated model cache. SegFormer-B2 uses the
pretrained `nvidia/mit-b2` weights and likewise needs access/cache.
For Kaggle, set `HF_HOME=/kaggle/working/hf-cache` if the default model cache
is not writable. The MiT-B2 encoder is ImageNet-pretrained; the binary
segmentation head is initialized for OTU_2D.

## Quick start

```bash
# 1. Audit before training (no torch required)
python -m scripts.audit_data --data-root /path/to/OTU_2D \
  --generated-root /path/to/generated_dataset_full

# 2. Train B0 baseline
python -m scripts.train --experiment B0 --model unet \
  --data-root /path/to/OTU_2D --seed 42

# 3. Train SoDAR global meta-lambda on the same split
python -m scripts.train --experiment S4 --model unet \
  --data-root /path/to/OTU_2D \
  --generated-root /path/to/generated_dataset_full --seed 42

# 4. Test one finalized checkpoint (do not test during model search)
python -m scripts.evaluate --checkpoint runs/S4_unet_seed42/best.pt
```

See **[RUN.md](RUN.md)** for the complete matrix, Kaggle paths, three seeds,
phantom test and troubleshooting. See **[docs/METHOD.md](docs/METHOD.md)** for
losses and the meaning/limitations of meta-learning.

For the SIGM workstation, `configs/sigm.yaml` already sets
`/mnt/nvme0/home/sonbh/Dataset/OTU_2D` as the shared parent of `images` and
`annotations`. Start with:

```bash
python -m scripts.audit_data --config configs/sigm.yaml --real-only
```

## Auditability and safeguards

- All runs store `config.json`, `history.json`, and best validation checkpoint.
- Train and validation use separate manifest lists; test runs in its own CLI.
- Report macro *per-image* Dice/IoU alongside global micro-Dice; retain eight
  image-level categories for stratified reporting.
- Three seeds are predeclared: `42 123 2026` (override via `--seeds`).
- Data preprocessing is identical in every comparison; *only* the specified
  augmentation or synthetic supervision differs.
- The S3 naive lambda baseline should mathematically collapse toward zero;
  it is intentionally included as a negative control.
- The first-order meta step uses a **separate real minibatch from train**, not
  the 150-image validation set. It is a pragmatic gradient-based approximation,
  **not** a fully independent validation-based bilevel estimator.
- This is a **research prototype**. Validate original images, annotation
  semantics, duplicates, patient IDs and the DDPM's source split separately.

## References and attribution

- Zhao et al., *MMOTU*, https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.06799
- Luong et al., *SovaSeg-Net*, ICIP 2024, https://github.com/SonBH0410/SovaSeg-Net
- Moon et al., *DAKTer*, IEEE TCSVT 2026, DOI: 10.1109/TCSVT.2026.3701691
- Ren et al., *Learning to Reweight Examples*, ICML 2018,
  https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/ren18a.html
- Liu et al., *Auto-Lambda*, TMLR 2022, https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.03091
- Hinton et al., *Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network*,
  https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.02531

SoDAR adapts known weighting ideas to the particular **negative transfer from
synthetic soft-label supervision**. A learnable scalar alone is not a novelty
claim. The study must isolate, measure and substantiate its actual benefit.
